				<table cellpadding=5 cellspacing=0 border=0>
					<tr>
						<td style="font-size: 14pt; font-weight: bold" colspan="2">
							Gunman Surrenders After Fla. Mail Hijack
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
						<td style="font-size: 11pt;"> 
							
							<a href="#" OnClick="openpic('photos/MAIL_TRUCK_CHASE8.jpg')"><IMG SRC="/DSDSMULTIMEDIA/photos/MAIL_TRUCK_CHASE8.jpg" width="250" border="0" align="left"></a>
							A police sniper, right, keeps an eye on a postal truck where a gunman is holding a postal carrier hostage Friday, Jan. 31, 2003 in Miami. (AP Photo/Alan Diaz)
							 
							<br>
							<br>
							08:30 AM EST February 01, 2003 
							<br>
							The Associated Press<br><br><br>MIAMI<p class="nitfp">A gunman seized a mail truck Friday and forced the postal carrier to lead police on a 90-minute low-speed chase through the streets of Miami before he finally released his hostage and surrendered.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Surrounded by heavily armed police, the gunman backed slowly out of the truck with his hands over his head, ending a five-hour drama that was covered by TV helicopters and broadcast nationally.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Miami-Dade County police chief Carlos Alvarez said officials were in constant communication with the gunman, identified as Nevia Abraham.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"He was just rambling, he wasn't making any demands," Alvarez said.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Abraham, 38, released mail carrier Tanya Mitchell about an hour before he gave up, police said. Mitchell, apparently unharmed, climbed out of the truck and ran to police.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The chase began after Abraham seized the mail truck and fled, firing shots at pursuing officers, Miami-Dade County police Detective Randy Rossman said. Police said Abraham was armed with two guns.</p>
<p class="nitfp">A second man, Jonathan Hamilton, 47, was also arrested.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Officials said Abraham would be charged with attempted murder, kidnapping and armed robbery, while Hamilton is facing armed robbery charges.</p>
<p class="nitfp">As the truck wound its way through the city's streets with the hijacker inside, more than a dozen police cruisers chased it. Police eventually stopped the truck by placing spikes in the road to flatten its tires.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The truck stopped in a busy intersection, and Mitchell was then moved to the back of the truck. Police said Abraham partially blocked the passenger window with mail. Police later sent a remote-controlled robot to hand the gunman a phone.</p>
<p class="nitfp">After a standoff of more than two hours, Mitchell climbed out.</p>
<p class="nitfp">During the chase, the truck traveled at about the speed limit and stopped periodically near bystanders while someone inside spoke to them.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The Rev. Marc Cooper said he gave his cell phone to a man inside the truck at one point, hoping that police could communicate with him.</p>
<p class="nitfp">"He didn't seem agitated ... he seemed calm," Cooper told WSVN-TV.</p>
<p class="nitfp">The mail carrier had been delivering mail on her route when she was carjacked, postal inspector Manny Gonzalez told WFOR-TV. He said carriers are trained to deal with hostage situations.</p>
<p class="nitfp">Forty-eight Miami-Dade County schools were locked down during the chase as a precaution.</p>

